1
00:00:00,211 --> 00:00:04,199
The night before Challenger launched,
its engineers said it was too cold to fly.

2
00:00:05,241 --> 00:00:06,215
They were overruled.

3
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:09,168
This is the story of mission 51-L.

4
00:00:16,311 --> 00:00:19,229
Challenger was the second
space shuttle to enter service,

5
00:00:19,741 --> 00:00:22,619
and it first flew in April 1983.

6
00:00:23,861 --> 00:00:27,301
On that first mission, the crew
launched a communications satellite,

7
00:00:27,492 --> 00:00:29,989
and made the first spacewalk
of the shuttle program.

8
00:00:31,131 --> 00:00:34,831
By January 1986 it had
completed nine missions,

9
00:00:35,282 --> 00:00:37,939
and mission 51-L was its tenth.

10
00:00:39,041 --> 00:00:42,358
NASA had promised that the shuttle
would make spaceflight routine,

11
00:00:42,549 --> 00:00:47,170
and in 1985 it was planning
24 flights a year by 1990.

12
00:00:48,170 --> 00:00:51,004
Mission 51-L would
also carry a teacher.

13
00:00:51,596 --> 00:00:53,448
In July 1985,

14
00:00:53,518 --> 00:00:56,733
Vice President George Bush
announced that Christa McAuliffe,

15
00:00:56,823 --> 00:00:58,175
of Concord, New Hampshire,

16
00:00:58,406 --> 00:01:01,280
had been chosen from more
than 11,000 applicants.

17
00:01:02,321 --> 00:01:06,888
She trained with NASA, including
weightless flights on a KC-135 aircraft.

18
00:01:08,171 --> 00:01:11,739
She was to teach lessons from orbit,
to classrooms across the country.

19
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:16,288
Another teacher, Barbara Morgan,
trained beside her as her backup.

20
00:01:17,430 --> 00:01:20,306
The crew of seven also included
commander Dick Scobee,

21
00:01:20,717 --> 00:01:23,532
pilot Michael Smith, Ellison Onizuka,

22
00:01:23,842 --> 00:01:27,419
Judith Resnik, Ronald
McNair, and Gregory Jarvis.

23
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:31,596
The launch was planned
for the 22nd of January.

24
00:01:32,168 --> 00:01:33,962
It was postponed three times,

25
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:37,329
and then scrubbed on the 27th
because of high crosswinds.

26
00:01:41,651 --> 00:01:45,542
The forecast for that night called for
temperatures in the low twenties,

27
00:01:45,562 --> 00:01:48,019
perhaps as low as 18
degrees Fahrenheit.

28
00:01:49,161 --> 00:01:53,279
The solid rocket boosters were
built by Morton Thiokol, in Utah.

29
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:55,524
Each was assembled from segments,

30
00:01:55,754 --> 00:01:58,089
joined by seals made
of rubber O-rings.

31
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:03,867
Thiokol engineer Roger Boisjoly had
already warned about those joints.

32
00:02:04,438 --> 00:02:08,493
In a memo in July 1985, he wrote
that the result of a failure would be,

33
00:02:08,624 --> 00:02:12,699
quote, a catastrophe of the
highest order, loss of human life.

34
00:02:13,901 --> 00:02:18,740
In January 1985, a shuttle launched
with its O-rings at 53 degrees,

35
00:02:19,011 --> 00:02:20,343
the coldest to that date.

36
00:02:20,934 --> 00:02:23,529
Afterward, erosion was
found in both boosters.

37
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:28,660
At 8:45 that evening, engineers
and managers at Thiokol,

38
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:30,173
at Marshall Space Flight Center,

39
00:02:30,304 --> 00:02:32,839
and at Kennedy Space Center
joined a teleconference.

40
00:02:33,881 --> 00:02:37,699
Thiokol's engineers showed charts
of O-ring damage on earlier flights.

41
00:02:38,371 --> 00:02:42,010
Their recommendation was not to
launch below 53 degrees Fahrenheit.

42
00:02:43,232 --> 00:02:45,268
NASA's Marshall
managers pushed back.

43
00:02:45,941 --> 00:02:48,499
One of them, George Hardy,
said he was appalled.

44
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:53,281
Larry Mulloy later testified that
he may have said something like:

45
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:55,849
when do you want me
to launch, next April?

46
00:02:57,111 --> 00:03:00,351
Thiokol asked for a few minutes
off the line to talk privately.

47
00:03:00,782 --> 00:03:02,919
The break lasted about 30 minutes.

48
00:03:04,061 --> 00:03:05,564
Boisjoly later testified

49
00:03:05,584 --> 00:03:09,322
that a senior manager told Thiokol's
vice president of engineering

50
00:03:09,372 --> 00:03:12,569
to take off his engineering hat
and put on his management hat.

51
00:03:13,631 --> 00:03:17,259
Boisjoly said that no engineer
spoke in favor of launching.

52
00:03:18,241 --> 00:03:21,188
When the teleconference
resumed, at about 11 p.m.,

53
00:03:21,208 --> 00:03:24,226
Thiokol's management
reversed its position,

54
00:03:24,397 --> 00:03:25,609
and recommended the launch.

55
00:03:29,691 --> 00:03:34,378
On the morning of the 28th, ice hung
from the launch tower at pad 39B.

56
00:03:35,821 --> 00:03:37,455
Rockwell, which built the orbiter,

57
00:03:37,666 --> 00:03:40,282
told NASA that the ice was
an unknown condition,

58
00:03:40,433 --> 00:03:42,649
and that it could not
assure it was safe to fly.

59
00:03:43,851 --> 00:03:46,749
The temperature at launch
was 36 degrees Fahrenheit,

60
00:03:47,121 --> 00:03:49,678
15 degrees colder than
any previous launch.

61
00:03:51,082 --> 00:03:53,829
Challenger lifted off at
11:38 in the morning.

62
00:03:54,831 --> 00:03:56,405
Less than one second later,

63
00:03:56,535 --> 00:03:58,722
cameras recorded a
puff of gray smoke

64
00:03:58,742 --> 00:04:01,379
at the aft joint of the right
solid rocket booster.

65
00:04:02,721 --> 00:04:06,549
Investigators later concluded that
the seal in that joint had failed.

66
00:04:07,492 --> 00:04:09,717
For almost a minute,
the shuttle climbed.

67
00:04:11,101 --> 00:04:14,520
At about 59 seconds, a flame
appeared at the same joint.

68
00:04:15,071 --> 00:04:18,089
Within two seconds, it was
striking the external tank.

69
00:04:19,371 --> 00:04:22,331
At 73 seconds,
Challenger broke apart.

70
00:04:23,064 --> 00:04:24,799
All seven crew members were lost.

71
00:04:25,861 --> 00:04:26,583
That evening,

72
00:04:26,603 --> 00:04:29,442
President Reagan postponed
his State of the Union address,

73
00:04:29,633 --> 00:04:31,709
and spoke to the nation
from the Oval Office.

74
00:04:32,711 --> 00:04:33,697
He spoke directly

75
00:04:33,717 --> 00:04:35,738
to the schoolchildren
who had been watching.

76
00:04:36,930 --> 00:04:39,270
And I want to say something to the schoolchildren of America

77
00:04:39,270 --> 00:04:41,990
who were watching the live coverage of the shuttle's takeoff.

78
00:04:43,030 --> 00:04:47,130
I know it's hard to understand, but sometimes painful things like this happen.

79
00:04:51,311 --> 00:04:55,473
On the 31st of January, Reagan spoke
at a memorial service in Houston,

80
00:04:55,764 --> 00:04:57,499
and read the name of
each crew member.

81
00:04:58,890 --> 00:05:00,470
We remember Christa McAuliffe,

82
00:05:01,570 --> 00:05:03,910
who captured the imagination of the entire nation,

83
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:08,870
inspiring us with her pluck, her restless spirit of discovery,

84
00:05:10,091 --> 00:05:12,369
On the 3rd of February,
he named a commission,

85
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:15,239
led by William Rogers, a
former Secretary of State.

86
00:05:16,301 --> 00:05:19,180
Its members included Neil
Armstrong, Sally Ride,

87
00:05:19,411 --> 00:05:22,109
and the Nobel Prize winning
physicist Richard Feynman.

88
00:05:23,271 --> 00:05:25,685
At a public hearing on
the 11th of February,

89
00:05:26,056 --> 00:05:28,501
Feynman reported what
happened when he put a piece

90
00:05:28,521 --> 00:05:30,776
of the booster seal
rubber into ice water:

91
00:05:31,207 --> 00:05:32,479
it did not spring back.

92
00:05:33,961 --> 00:05:36,196
The Commission's
report, delivered in June,

93
00:05:36,407 --> 00:05:38,692
said the cause was the
failure of the pressure seal

94
00:05:38,712 --> 00:05:42,049
in the aft field joint of the
right solid rocket motor.

95
00:05:43,190 --> 00:05:46,727
The Commission found a serious
flaw in the decision making process.

96
00:05:47,338 --> 00:05:50,694
Had the engineers' doubts been
clearly stated and emphasized,

97
00:05:50,844 --> 00:05:53,879
it said, the launch might not
have occurred when it did.

98
00:05:55,161 --> 00:05:56,904
At Marshall, a launch constraint

99
00:05:56,924 --> 00:05:59,799
on the booster joints had been
waived six times in a row.

100
00:06:00,390 --> 00:06:02,604
The Commission found
that the senior managers,

101
00:06:02,774 --> 00:06:05,729
Jesse Moore and Arnold
Aldrich, did not know.

102
00:06:06,871 --> 00:06:08,946
It called the joint a faulty design,

103
00:06:09,318 --> 00:06:13,278
unacceptably sensitive to a number
of factors, including temperature.

104
00:06:14,721 --> 00:06:15,712
It also found

105
00:06:15,822 --> 00:06:18,928
that Thiokol's management
had reversed its position

106
00:06:18,948 --> 00:06:20,100
at Marshall's urging,

107
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:22,404
contrary to its engineers' views,

108
00:06:22,875 --> 00:06:25,409
in order to accommodate
a major customer.

109
00:06:26,691 --> 00:06:30,287
On the ice, the Commission found
the decision to launch questionable,

110
00:06:30,518 --> 00:06:33,062
because NASA appeared to
be requiring a contractor

111
00:06:33,082 --> 00:06:35,036
to prove it was not safe to launch,

112
00:06:35,306 --> 00:06:36,859
rather than proving that it was.

113
00:06:38,161 --> 00:06:41,377
Its first recommendation was that
the faulty joint must be changed,

114
00:06:41,868 --> 00:06:45,064
and that no design option should
be ruled out because of schedule,

115
00:06:45,194 --> 00:06:47,769
cost, or reliance on existing hardware.

116
00:06:49,050 --> 00:06:50,002
In his own appendix,

117
00:06:50,393 --> 00:06:52,457
Feynman wrote that
estimates of the chance

118
00:06:52,477 --> 00:06:55,331
of failure ranged from about 1 in 100,

119
00:06:55,662 --> 00:06:59,880
from working engineers, to 1 in
100,000, from management.

120
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:02,483
Feynman noted that odds of 1

121
00:07:02,503 --> 00:07:05,448
in 100,000 would mean
launching a shuttle every day

122
00:07:05,468 --> 00:07:07,241
for 300 years to lose one,

123
00:07:07,732 --> 00:07:09,685
and asked what lay
behind management's,

124
00:07:09,835 --> 00:07:12,450
quote, fantastic faith
in the machinery.

125
00:07:13,491 --> 00:07:17,669
He ended with a line that became
famous: for a successful technology,

126
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,815
reality must take precedence
over public relations,

127
00:07:20,966 --> 00:07:22,559
for nature cannot be fooled.

128
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:26,450
Was this an accident that did not have to happen?

129
00:07:27,430 --> 00:07:28,350
Yes, it was.

130
00:07:28,590 --> 00:07:33,350
It was an accident that had many, many warnings

131
00:07:33,350 --> 00:07:37,650
that there was something wrong and that it might sooner or later go off,

132
00:07:38,330 --> 00:07:40,370
and the warnings were disregarded.

133
00:07:41,631 --> 00:07:42,924
On the first anniversary,

134
00:07:43,356 --> 00:07:44,078
Reagan spoke

135
00:07:44,098 --> 00:07:47,839
to NASA employees about the lessons
Christa McAuliffe had hoped to teach.

136
00:07:49,110 --> 00:07:52,090
Well, she never got to give those lessons in space science,

137
00:07:52,770 --> 00:07:56,410
but she and the others did teach America's children something nevertheless,

138
00:07:57,110 --> 00:07:59,310
something precious, something enduring,

139
00:08:00,331 --> 00:08:02,646
The shuttle was
grounded for 32 months.

140
00:08:03,237 --> 00:08:07,236
Discovery flew again on
the 29th of September 1988,

141
00:08:07,326 --> 00:08:08,799
with redesigned boosters.

142
00:08:10,941 --> 00:08:13,901
The engineers said not to
launch below 53 degrees,

143
00:08:14,092 --> 00:08:16,269
and that same night the
warning was reversed.

144
00:08:17,461 --> 00:08:19,000
When the people closest
to the data say stop,

145
00:08:20,512 --> 00:08:22,489
who decides whether anyone listens?
